From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brett-Michael Green <brett@bmgvisualtech.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:51:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: zuma: Add CPU DVFS and thermal
 zones

Run the three CPU clusters on their ACPM DVFS clocks (cpucl0-2) through
cpufreq-dt, and add thermal zones for them on the ACPM TMU's big, mid
and little sensors (0-2):

- the operating points are the ACPM firmware's own levels, read from
  the ECT the bootloader leaves at 0x90000000, up to each cluster's
  max-freq in stock's exynos-acme domains: 1704 MHz little, 2367 MHz
  mid and 2914 MHz big. The ECT's little levels up to 1704 MHz are the
  ones stock's cpufreq statistics list;
- the energy model is stock's: each level's opp-microwatt is its power
  in the default pixel-em profile of Google's zuma-b0-ipop.dts, which
  the Pixel 8 Pro's own device tree has too. A dynamic power
  coefficient can't be used, since the firmware sets the voltages and
  there is no opp-microvolt: with one, the energy model fails to
  register ("EM: invalid perf. state: -22"). The CPUs get #cooling-cells
  for cpufreq cooling;
- the levels above stock's battery current limiter caps (1945 MHz mid,
  2294 MHz big, from Google's zuma-shusky-bcl.dtsi) are turbo-mode, as
  on gs201: with no BCL driver nothing pulls the clusters down when the
  battery voltage droops, so they are only used with boost enabled.
  Those caps are the Pixel 8 Pro's, so the tables stay in zuma.dtsi but
  only zuma-husky.dts points its CPUs at them: the Pixel 8a's caps in
  Google's zuma-akita-bcl.dtsi are lower, and it needs its own;
- a CPU zone throttles its cluster from 80 C, forces its lowest level
  from 95 C and is critical at 105 C, as zumapro.dtsi does. The TMU
  sensor and the zones are shared by all zuma boards.

The ACPM clock ids header moves from the board to zuma.dtsi.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Brett-Michael Green <brett@bmgvisualtech.com>
